  "report_sha256": "f856409b5a58b402ccf6f545dc8570cae22952a79b2b06460a8f68ab2635e10b",
  "scope": "Four independently acquired bounded sequences use identical workers and budgets. Both methods retain the same checked inventory and full maintenance state, pay initial full global coverage and selection, and query all originals after each batch. Only final catalog rebuilding differs. Cold replay checks each stored operation and every prefix against complete fresh catalogs, with fresh discovery disabled. Initial global query is independently recomputed once and each distinct batch version once; all saved global queries, selections and certificates must match. Reference search is disabled during validation. Full-query guards preserve admission ledgers and hold SQLite ownership, but rely on immutable caller-owned in-memory objects; they are not cryptographic checks of every cached byte. Warm catalog sharing adds no persistent index and removes a transient second catalog; cache counts are not peak RAM. Complete sequence charges copying, initial checks, worker preparation/acquisition/export/checking, union merge/check, global queries and result serialization. Post-sequence full catalog audits, final global reconstruction, reference checks and shared parent replay are separately charged. Two reversed-order repetitions are descriptive; the initial global-check variance prevents attributing the entire sequence difference to the catalog change. Producer timing precedes cold validation, tests, build and preservation checks; validation tasks overlap and their elapsed times are not performance comparisons.",
